BCC_Q({"lv": "910", "y": 2019, "n": 10, "part": "B", "topic": "logic", "title": "Escape Room", "answer": 1, "story": "<p>An escape room uses the following design for the inner-workings of a\nlock. The lock is made using nine rods and five blocks. Each\nblock has one or two input rods coming in from the left and one output\nrod coming out to the right. <\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"A description of the diagram follows.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:50.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<button onclick=\"hideSeek('longdesc9')\" style=\"box-shadow:none\">Hide/Reveal Description of Diagram for Escape Room<\/button>\n<div id=\"longdesc9\" style=\"display:none\">\n<p>Five blocks are arranged into three columns. The first two columns have two blocks. The last column has one block. Five of the nine rods are labelled with letters (P, Q, R, S, and T).<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first column has a green block on the top and a red block on the bottom. \n  <ul>\n<li>The green block has two input rods, labelled P and Q, and one output rod.<\/li>\n<li>The red block has one input rod, labelled R, and one output rod.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<li>The second column has a red block on the top and a green block on the bottom.\n  <ul>\n<li>The red block has one input rod, which is the output rod of the green top block in the first column, and one output rod.<\/li>\n<li>The green block has two input rods, which are the output rod of the red bottom block in the first column and the rod labelled S, and one output rod.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<li>The third column has a blue block. The blue block has two input rods, which are the output rods of the red and green blocks in the second column, and one output rod, labelled T.<\/li>\n<\/li><\/li><\/ol>\n<hr class=\"longdesc\"/>\n<\/div>\n<p>Each rod turns like a key either clockwise or counter-clockwise. The table below describes how input rods\nand the type of block determine how output rods turn.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Block<\/th>\n<th>Result<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><span><img \"style=\"width:150px\" alt=\"Blue block with two inputs and one output\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"/><\/span><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">If both input rods are turned clockwise,\n    then the output rod will turn clockwise. Otherwise, the output rod will\n    turn counter-clockwise.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><span><img \"style=\"width:150px\" alt=\"Green block with two inputs and one output\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"/><\/span><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">If both input rods are turned\ncounter-clockwise, then the output rod will turn counter-clockwise.\nOtherwise, the output rod will turn clockwise.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><span><img \"style=\"width:150px\" alt=\"Red block with one input and one output\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"/><\/span><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The output rod will turn in the opposite\ndirection as the input rod.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>", "question": "<p>Each input rod at P, Q, R, and S is turned once causing the output\nrod at T to turn clockwise. How might have the input rods been\nturned?<\/p>\n<ol class=\"mc\">\n<li>P clockwise, Q clockwise, R counter-clockwise, S\nclockwise<\/li>\n<li>P counter-clockwise, Q counter-clockwise, R counter-clockwise, S\nclockwise<\/li>\n<li>P clockwise, Q counter-clockwise, R counter-clockwise, S\nclockwise<\/li>\n<li>P counter-clockwise, Q counter-clockwise, R clockwise, S\ncounter-clockwise<\/li>\n<\/ol>", "answerHtml": "<p>(B) P counter-clockwise, Q counter-clockwise, R counter-clockwise, S\nclockwise<\/p>", "explanation": "<p>One way to solve this problem is to work backwards, starting with the\noutput rod at T.<\/p>\n<p>In order for T to turn clockwise, the input rods of the <img alt=\"Blue\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80px\"/> block must both turn clockwise.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:60.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>For the output rod of the top <img alt=\"Red\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:100px\"/> block to turn clockwise, the input\nrod must turn counter-clockwise.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:60.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>For the output rod of the top <img alt=\"Green\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80px\"/> block to turn counter-clockwise,\nboth input rods must turn counter-clockwise.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<p><img alt=\"Input rods labelled P and Q both turn counter-clockwise.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:75.0%\"/><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>This eliminates Options A and C. One way for the bottom <img alt=\"Green\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80px\"/> block to turn clockwise is for both input rods to turn clockwise. This would mean\nthat S turns clockwise and R turns counter-clockwise, which is Option\nB.<\/p>\n<p>The other way for the bottom <img alt=\"Green\" class=\"static\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:80px\"/> block to turn clockwise is for both\ninput rods to turn in opposite directions. This would mean that S and R\nturn in the same direction. This eliminates Option D.<\/p>", "connections": "<p>All computers contain <em>circuits<\/em>. Circuits are built using\nmany small components called <em>logic gates<\/em>. A logic gate takes in\n<em>binary input<\/em> and produces <em>binary output<\/em>. Binary data\nis simply data with two possibilities often considered 1 or 0 or\nsometimes <em>true<\/em> or <em>false<\/em>. In this problem, our binary\ndata is “clockwise\" or “counter-clockwise\". The escape room lock is like\na circuit where the blocks are the gates and binary data is transferred\nusing the rods.<\/p>\n<p>In this problem, the specific gates in the table are commonly called\nAND, OR, and NOT respectively. Their meaning corresponds to the formal\nand mathematical definitions of the words “and\", “or\", and “not\". It is\nan amazing fact that everything a modern computer can do, can be\nachieved by connecting gates in clever ways.<\/p>"});